WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.800
Why is Zero Trust a whole-agency property?

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.050
Look at the usual assessment calendar.

3
00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:08.575
The network team reviews the
boundary, keyed by address.

4
00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:13.575
The cloud team runs each provider's
posture scan, keyed by resource tags.

5
00:00:13.575 --> 00:00:16.781
The virtualization team
works a hypervisor checklist,

6
00:00:16.781 --> 00:00:19.987
keyed by whatever the management
plane calls a machine.

7
00:00:19.987 --> 00:00:23.050
At year end someone puts
the three reports behind

8
00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:26.038
one cover page and calls
it the agency's maturity.

9
00:00:26.038 --> 00:00:28.775
It's three partial
assessments with one title.

10
00:00:28.775 --> 00:00:30.725
The failure is architectural.

11
00:00:30.725 --> 00:00:36.104
A database server that runs as a virtual
machine, sits on an agency subnet and backs

12
00:00:36.104 --> 00:00:41.325
up to a cloud bucket can appear as three
unrelated objects, scored by three tools.

13
00:00:41.325 --> 00:00:44.725
A total across them counts
objects, not architecture.

14
00:00:44.725 --> 00:00:47.850
The guidance describes the
enterprise, not a tenant.

15
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:52.825
Executive Order 14028 says throughout
all aspects of the infrastructure,

16
00:00:52.825 --> 00:01:00.688
and NIST SP 800-207 grants no implicit
trust from network location or ownership.

17
00:01:00.688 --> 00:01:04.570
So unify first, then assess,
and defend the unified plane,

18
00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:08.520
because it becomes a complete
map of the attack surface.
